# Work sessions

This folder is the base path for the user's work. It is synced to OneDrive and is
the source for the audit trail, so its layout matters.

## Every request gets its own folder

**Do not write files into the root of this folder.** When the user asks for a new
piece of work, create a folder for it first and do everything inside that folder:

```
work_sessions/YYYY/MM/DD/HH-mm-ss_short-slug/
```

Name it after the request: `2026/09/09/14-05-00_vnet-peering-issue`, local time of
creation, lowercase, dashes for spaces, no punctuation. **No colons** in the time —
OneDrive rejects them. If the user starts a clearly separate piece of work later
in the same session, make a new folder for it rather than reusing the current one.

Announce the folder you created so the user can find it.

## Layout

| path | holds |
|---|---|
| `YYYY/MM/DD/HH-mm-ss_slug/` | one request: notes, scripts, data, outputs |
| `YYYY/MM/DD/HH-mm-ss_slug/.session.json` | name, profile, ticket, task_type (the folder's default), session_types (per-session overrides), started_at, ended_at |
| `_audit/` | CSV exports from `claude-audit`; underscore keeps it out of `claude-new -l` |
| `_config/` | claude-worksessions settings: `config.env`, `context.md` (the standing context below), `review-rules.md`, `pinned.json` (the VS Code sidebar's pins). The standing context below is copied in from `_config/context.md` — edit it there, then run `install.sh` to regenerate this file |
| `_audit/moved-folders.json` | old → new path map from the 2026-09-18 move off flat `YYYY-MM-DD_slug/`. `claude-audit` and `claude-search` resolve transcript `cwd`s through it — don't delete it; add to it if folders are ever moved again |

Folders beginning with `_` are infrastructure, not requests.

## How sessions are meant to start

`claude-new -t TICKET "topic"` (from claude-worksessions, `shell/worksessions.zsh`, sourced
from `~/.zshrc`) creates the `YYYY/MM/DD/HH-mm-ss_slug` folder, records the profile and the **Jira
ticket** (mandatory: `PREFIX-123` such as `{{CWS_TICKET_EXAMPLE}}`, or `Other`), cd's in, and launches Claude.
`-p PROFILE` skips the profile prompt (`claude-new -L` lists them); a leading `{{CWS_TICKET_EXAMPLE}}` is taken as the ticket;
`claude-new -l` lists recent sessions with their ticket.
`-n` / `--no-audit` writes `"audit": false` to `.session.json`; `claude-audit` (and so the
weekly review and daily recap) then skips that session. `claude-search` still finds it.
For a session with no request folder (e.g. started in the root), add its session id to
`_audit/no-audit.txt` instead — flagging the root would hide every root session.

`.session.json` also carries a **task type** — what kind of work this is
(permissions, job errors, new features, security, ...). `claude-new` suggests one from
the session name and from the types already in use.

**The type is what started the work, not everything it touches.** A job error that
needs investigation, then a permission change, then a documentation update is
`job errors` throughout — the permissions and documentation work only exists because
the job failed. Type the trigger, not the detours.

**If the work clearly moves to a different kind of task, change it**: run
`claude-type "<type>"` in the session folder and say in one line that you did. That sets
the type for **this session only** — the folder keeps its own default for the next one,
and `claude-type --folder "<type>"` changes that default instead. Reuse an existing type
where one fits (`claude-type` with no argument shows the current one and where it came
from); invent a new one only when nothing fits. Change it only when the **trigger**
turns out to have been something else — what looked like a job error was really a
permissions request all along — never because the work passed through another type on
the way.

The ticket is what lets the weekly functional review put a session on the right
tracker row without asking. If a session sitting in a request folder has no
ticket in its `.session.json` and the work clearly belongs to one, say so.

If a session was started some other way and is sitting in this root, create the
request folder yourself before writing anything.

## Weekly functional review

The `weekly-review` skill fills the weekly tracker from sessions, Teams, calendar,
mail, out-of-office and Jira. Its decisions live in `_audit/review/ledger.json` —
never edit that by hand; record changes through `claude-audit --apply`.

## Auditing

`claude-audit [--day|--week|--month|--from/--to] [--detail|--reconcile] [--copy|--csv]` shows the
tracker summary or every activity for any period. It reconstructs the trail from the Claude transcripts — it does not
read this folder, so nothing here needs to be maintained by hand. Profile
attribution comes from `.session.json`, which is why sessions should start via `claude-new`.

`claude-search QUERY [--ai] [--from/--to]` finds past sessions on a ticket or topic,
with the command to resume each. When the user asks where or when they worked on
something, run it rather than grepping transcripts.

